2025-05-01 01:43:58 - Thread-426 (execute) - INFO - [Exploration]
Relevant columns, their purpose, and typical conditions  

• bikeshare_trips  
  – `trip_id`: record identifier (inspect a few rows)  
  – `bike_number`: bike we want to total‐up (GROUP BY target)  
  – `start_station_id`, `end_station_id`: where the ride began/ended (JOIN key)  
  – `start_date`, `end_date`: optional time filtering (none needed for totals)  

• bikeshare_stations  
  – `station_id`: JOIN key to trips’ station ids  
  – `latitude`, `longitude`: coordinates that let us turn each station into a GEOGRAPHY point and measure distance  
  (No filtering needed; we only JOIN on station_id)  

General idea  
1. JOIN every trip to its start‑station coordinates and to its end‑station coordinates.  
2. Build a GEOGRAPHY point for each end and start.  
3. Use ST_DISTANCE(start_point , end_point) to get metres travelled per trip.  
4. SUM that per `bike_number` to obtain total metres (or kilometres) for each bike.  

Below are 10 exploratory → final BigQuery SQL statements (each limited to 20 rows) to understand the data and then produce the whole answer.  

```sql
-- 1. Peek at trips
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. List a few distinct bikes that appear in the trips table
SELECT DISTINCT bike_number
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips`
ORDER BY bike_number
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Inspect the station table (coordinates, etc.)
SELECT station_id, name, latitude, longitude
FROM `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Join one side only: attach *start*‑station coordinates to trips
SELECT t.trip_id,
       t.start_station_id,
       s.latitude  AS start_lat,
       s.longitude AS start_lon
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips` t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s
  ON   t.start_station_id = s.station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Join both start & end stations for the same trip
SELECT t.trip_id,
       s1.latitude  AS start_lat,
       s1.longitude AS start_lon,
       s2.latitude  AS end_lat,
       s2.longitude AS end_lon
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips` t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s1
  ON   t.start_station_id = s1.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s2
  ON   t.end_station_id   = s2.station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Compute raw distance (metres) for each of those 20 example trips
SELECT t.trip_id,
       ST_DISTANCE(
         ST_GEOGPOINT(s1.longitude, s1.latitude),
         ST_GEOGPOINT(s2.longitude, s2.latitude)
       ) AS metres_travelled
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips` t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s1
  ON   t.start_station_id = s1.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s2
  ON   t.end_station_id   = s2.station_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Aggregate distance by bike for a small sample
SELECT t.bike_number,
       SUM(
         ST_DISTANCE(
           ST_GEOGPOINT(s1.longitude, s1.latitude),
           ST_GEOGPOINT(s2.longitude, s2.latitude)
         )
       ) AS total_metres
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips` t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s1
  ON   t.start_station_id = s1.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s2
  ON   t.end_station_id   = s2.station_id
GROUP  BY t.bike_number
ORDER  BY total_metres DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same aggregation but output kilometres (rounded) instead of metres
SELECT t.bike_number,
       ROUND(SUM(
         ST_DISTANCE(
           ST_GEOGPOINT(s1.longitude, s1.latitude),
           ST_GEOGPOINT(s2.longitude, s2.latitude)
         )
       ) / 1000, 2) AS total_km
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips` t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s1
  ON   t.start_station_id = s1.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s2
  ON   t.end_station_id   = s2.station_id
GROUP  BY t.bike_number
ORDER  BY total_km DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Verify that any trip with missing station coordinates is excluded
SELECT COUNT(*) AS trips_missing_stn
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips` t
LEFT  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s1
       ON t.start_station_id = s1.station_id
LEFT  JOIN `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s2
       ON t.end_station_id   = s2.station_id
WHERE  s1.station_id IS NULL
   OR  s2.station_id IS NULL;
```

```sql
-- 10. Final query: total distance travelled (km) by every bike in the system
--     (this is the one that fully answers the question)
SELECT
  t.bike_number,
  ROUND(SUM(
    ST_DISTANCE(
      ST_GEOGPOINT(s1.longitude, s1.latitude),
      ST_GEOGPOINT(s2.longitude, s2.latitude)
    )
  ) / 1000, 2) AS total_km_travelled
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_trips` t
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s1
  ON   t.start_station_id = s1.station_id
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.san_francisco.bikeshare_stations` s2
  ON   t.end_station_id   = s2.station_id
GROUP  BY t.bike_number
ORDER  BY total_km_travelled DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

Run query #10 without the `LIMIT` clause if you need the entire list of bikes and their total kilometres travelled.
